<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Příbor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Freiberg in Mähren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Příbor is a small town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, in the Moravian-Silesian Region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. Founded in the thirteenth century, it has a well-preserved historic centre and was long home to a Piarist college. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychoanalysis">psychoanalysis</a>, was born there in 1856, and his birthplace is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pribor.jpg" alt="Příbor" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Jurinek (Jiří Jurečka) at cs.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pribor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Příbor lies on the Lubina River in the Moravian-Silesian Foothills, about 10 kilometres east of Nový Jičín and 20 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a>. Its highest point is 370 metres above sea level. The town has about 8,300 inhabitants. Its name means cutlery in modern Czech, but this is only a coincidence. If the Czech name is older, it probably comes from při boru or při zboru, meaning at a pine forest or at a ruin. If the German name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> came first, it was originally rendered Vriburch or Freiburg and means free castle or castle with free access.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Count Frank von Hückeswagen founded the town in 1251, as a deed of the Moravian margrave Ottokar II confirmed in the same year, where it was called Vriburch. Its square was laid out on an old trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the count built a solid castle. His descendants held the town until 1359, when it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, who favoured it. Franz von Dietrichstein in particular granted it a number of privileges.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> the town burned down three times, most destructively in the Swedish invasion of 1643, and it recovered in the second half of the 17th century. In 1694 Karl II von Liechtenstein-Kastelkorn founded a Piarist monastery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, which also made education available to poorer people and made Příbor an important centre of education. From 1875 to 1938 the Czech Teacher Institute operated there and trained a number of significant teachers and figures of culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Příbor is one of the oldest towns in the region, and its historic centre is protected as an urban monument reservation. A regular square is lined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burgher</a> houses, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> alterations. The Baroque Piarist monastery now houses the town museum, a library and a primary art school. The most important of the town's four churches is the Church of the Nativity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, founded with the town, whose present brick building dates from the 14th century and has a tall slender tower. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>'s birthplace is a museum with an exhibition about his life and work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Příbor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mendel's work in Brno became a foundation of genetics after its rediscovery in 1900; Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first president of Czechoslovakia, was born in Hodonín in 1850 and Sigmund Freud in <b>Příbor</b> in 1856; and the composer Leoš Janáček made his career in Brno.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
